   "text": "I read the structure in four parts and then joined them into one macrocyclic SMILES.\n\n**Left side.** A 4-methylpiperazine is linked through N\u2013CH2CH2\u2013O to an ester C(=O). The \u03b1-carbon carries a hashed methyl and a CH2 that leads to a benzene ring. I assigned `[C@H]` there. The hashed methyl points back, so the implicit H points toward the viewer. With neighbours ordered C(=O), H, CH3, CH2, that gives `@`.\n\n**Central benzene.** It is meta-disubstituted. One substituent is the CH2 from the ester side. The other is a quaternary carbon carrying a wavy-bond methyl, so no stereo is assigned there.\n\n**Macrocycle.** The quaternary carbon joins three parts:\n- a chain of three CH2 groups, then C(CH3)2, then CH2, then SO2, then CH2CH2, ending at C4 of the indole;\n- the C3 carbon of a 1-methyl-1,2,4-triazole;\n- the central benzene already described.\n\n**Right side.**\n- The indole has its NH, a fluorine at C6 and an ether oxygen at C5. C4 carries the CH2CH2 chain.\n- The ether oxygen connects to a fluorophenyl ring. The fluorine on that ring is ortho to the carbon bearing the triazole, and the oxygen is meta to it.\n- The triazole C5 bears that fluorophenyl ring, and N1 bears the methyl.\n\nI checked the ring closures atom by atom against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: CN1CCN(CC1)CCOC(=O)[C@H](C)Cc1cccc(c1)C2(C)CCCC(C)(C)CS(=O)(=O)CCc3c4cc[nH]c4cc(F)c3Oc5ccc(F)c(c5)c6nc2nn6C"
